 of looks like blood bending from  Avatar The Last Airbender but that's  probably way way off but as I said this  isn't the main point of the arc and once  the scene meets its grim conclusion we  pick up with the straw hats once again  as they come across a ship that seems to  be on low morale know-how and supplies  all before they land on this new island  filled with long things I mean I write  for a living and I don't know how else  to best describe it there's a lot of  long things on this island long and tall  but what I liked about this opening was  that instead of setting a goal it poses  a question to the viewer  why was there a ship so ill-equipped out  there oftentimes stories like to  establish a goal of an ark right away  but doing this time it again can get  sort of predictable so establishing a  question is a nice change of pace for  this instance  once on this strange Island the crew  splits into two groups one led by Luffy  who bounds on to the island looking to  explore and the other half stay at the  ship to take care of it and both groups  encounter their own odd individuals  Luffy's group arrives and comes across a  house and help an old man off of some  super tall stills that he's been stuck  atop for 10 years which in and of itself  is ridiculous but what's more is that  his horse that shaped like a giraffe  waited for him all that time because he  loved him which gave me serious Futurama  vibes flashing me back to that time  Fry's dog waited for him until he died  though admittedly I thought that story  was much more sad which is good as I  don't think this old man character is  here for any other reason than to  provide the straw hat someone to protect  or to avenge as his horse ends up being  shot by the individuals which this arc  deals with primarily the group that  approaches the crew on the ship is  called the foxy pirates led by the  wonderfully designed foxy the Silver Fox  I'm always interested in seeing how  these overly cartoonish villains are  designed by Oda just by looking at this  guy you can tell exactly what he's about  and that's just the sign of a great  design and it's during this section we  are given an answer to that question  posed at the very beginning of this  story as well as given two separate  goals to work towards as a reader or  viewer help the old guy find his way  back to his tribe that left him behind  because he was on stills for 10 years  and defeat the foxy pirates in this  jewel day proposed after shooting his  horse in the leg now first things first  the duel more specifically this is  called a Davy back fight and in this  instance it's a three-part challenge  that includes a race a ball game and a  combat section and overall I enjoyed it  I thought it was somewhat predictable in  a lot of ways but there was just enough  for me to sink my teeth into with the  first two rounds with the third and  final round genuinely surprisingly for  the better but I'll get into that a  little bit later what makes these Davy  back fights interesting in the first  place are the rules and stakes that come  packaged with them interference is  allowed there are no substitutes and  whoever wins gets to select either the  flag of that crew or their crew member  to join your forces for keeps these  create much higher stakes than I  expected and as I pointed out earlier  the incentive to fight on behalf of the  old man was there for the characters  although I personally didn't have much  of a connection to him myself but hey  I'm willing to  some games of skill for fun and with  that the teams are divvied up round one  the donut race is Nico Robin Musa and  Nami round two the ball game or the  groggy ring C's Oro teaming up with  chopper and Sanji and finally round  three is a compact section which sees  monkey D Luffy versus the captain of the  foxy pirates silver fox and what's  interesting is that each of these  sections has something narrative Li  going on to help keep us engaged so  let