t we also lose  a valued crew member of the straw hats  and this is a particularly difficult  thing to watch as we've seen all of the  individual stories this ragtag group of  misfits have shown us  to see one of them get torn away from  them is heartbreaking specifically  because the one that ultimately gets  taken away is chopper and now it's time  to see if they can win him back  it's time for round two the groggy ring  with Zorro and Sanjay because chopper  was taken in the prior round the team of  Sanjay and Zorro are now faced with a  two on three disadvantage without the  ability to substitute once again light  the last round with the race the good  guy team is at an obvious disadvantage  from the get-go and that's ignoring the  more glaring aspects that are working  against them the imagery during this  introductory portion is fantastic shown  in a domineering fashion dwarfing the  straw hats  the imagery really communicates a David  versus Goliath type scenario and reminds  me a lot of the imagery from Dragon Ball  Akira Toriyama loved to draw kid Goku in  a very small way pitted up against these  massive opponents as this makes it all  the more satisfying to see when Goku  would eventually topple these imposing  foes but one aspect of this contest that  made no sense to me was the notion that  interference was allowed but they aren't  allowed to field a third player  what if someone consistently interfered  say like as much as a player in a match  would I mean I don't know I found that  to be sort of self contradictory but the  real story behind this particular round  isn't necessarily the straw hats  overcoming the enemy team in this  exchange though that's definitely part  of it the real story is instead made  more interesting because of Sanjay and  Zoro's notoriously ill-fitted dynamic  and that is very much the case in this  contest between getting on each other's  nerves and in each other's way the two  straw hats get beaten to a pulp  partially because they are being cheated  but also because they can't harmonize  but in a momentary truce for 10 seconds  their combined efforts humiliate and  destroy the opposing team which was  incredibly cathartic and they ended up  getting chopper back - which is always  good news which finally brings us to the  third and final round of the Davey back  fight monkey D Luffy versus captain's  silver fox the pomp and ceremony that  builds up this event between these two  in this final round is so funny  obviously after foxy threw underhanded  means fixes the location of the fight  stalls food March stands and even  bleachers all get set up as the two  begin to enter the ring stage to do  combat whoever knocks their opponent  outside of the 100 meter circle wins in  Luffy's quarter is Usopp and thanks to  his help Luffy comes  looking fittingly ridiculous Rousseff  also is the absolute perfect person to  have in Lupe's corner as a height man  and the way that he talks Luffy into  wearing an afro is honestly inspired but  if I'm being perfectly honest with this  final fight I wasn't expecting anything  significant I was expecting some  slapstick humour some quick thinking but  for Luffy to eventually get the better  of him and all that does happen on a  number of occasions as Luffy deals with  the unfair odds stacked against him by  this fittingly fiendish foxy tried to  overcome his ridiculously powerful devil  free ability and even more trouble below  deck involving a giant robot and a  mirror trick but what's interesting to  me anyway is the relentless nature of  this fight it begins in a light-hearted  place but eventually as the fight  escalates the bloody body of Luffy falls  on the floor time and time again and  typically this is where the rule of  three comes into effect typically  speaking a lot of story beats come in  threes as do jokes for that matter  usually with the third instance  something repeats there's a significant  difference that turns the tide of that  pattern but with this fight Luffy falls  to the floor way more than three times  and each and every time he drags himself  to his feet each time slower than the  last  suddenly as an audience we stop looking  at this fight as something light-hearted  and realized that Luffy is hurting  immensely and that's when we see this  throughout this chapter and fight  various characters make note of luffy's  deteriorating condition  foxy his crew and eventually even the  straw hats who up until this instance  had been firmly in the corner of Luffy  thinking that he could never lose to  this joker but when faced with their  friend their captain beaten into a  motionless heap on the floor even they  have to recognize it but once again  despite being on the verge of losing  more than just this match Luffy staggers  to his feet once again declaring without  a hint of reluctancy or uncertainty  I won't lose a single member of my crew  even if it kills me what an absolute  champion and with this defiance stand  before his maker legs shaking breathing  uneven but with an unmatched  determination spurring him on even Foxx  his crew began to get behind Luffy which  is a very sweet visual but as I said  it's breaking this rule of three that  makes us as an audience feel more  uneasy about this fight and reinforced  by the imagery as well as witnessing the  ones always nimble Luffy barely managed  to move without falling to the floor is  what he's hit by yet another attack this  time more massive than all the rest and  so seeing him stand once again starts to  feel uncomfortable  not to mention unpredictable especially  when you know the lengths he's willing  to go to and perhaps the most worrying  thing about this is that luffy was  genuinely at his limit and was about to  lose had it not been for a complete  fluke a random shard of the mirror from  earlier in the fight had become lodged  in his afro reflecting the beam back at  foxy before luffy had time to deal the  finishing blow finally